::: {.doc-subject}

Check if a set of nodes d-separates two others in a directed acyclic graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L65911-L66045){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.is_d_separator(
    x,
    y,
    z,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Is d-separator](../assets/graphs/is-d-separator.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="A causal graph: smoking (teal) causes tar, tar causes cancer (violet), and smoking also causes yellow fingers. Left: conditioning on tar (outlined) blocks the only path from smoking to cancer, so is_d_separator returns True. Right: with nothing conditioned on, the chain smoking, tar, cancer (accent) is open, so smoking and cancer are dependent and is_d_separator returns False." width="100%"}

In a causal diagram or Bayesian network (a DAG whose edges point from causes to
effects), d-separation reads conditional independence off the graph: if `z` d-separates
`x` from `y`, then `x` and `y` are independent given `z` in every probability
distribution that respects the graph. A path between them is *blocked* by `z` when it
passes through a chain or fork node in `z`, or through a collider (a node with both
path edges pointing into it) that is not in `z` and has no descendant in `z`.
`z` d-separates `x` and `y` when every path is blocked. This is the basis of choosing
adjustment sets in causal inference and of testing a causal model against data.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[x]{.doc-parameter-name}</code>

:   Nodes, or lists of nodes, as IDs or labels.

<code>[y]{.doc-parameter-name}</code>

:   Nodes, or lists of nodes, as IDs or labels.

<code>[z]{.doc-parameter-name}</code>

:   The conditioning nodes (a node, a list, or an empty list).
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   `True` if `z` d-separates `x` from `y`.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If the graph is not a directed acyclic graph, a node does not exist, or the three
    sets are not disjoint.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

The check is a "Bayes ball" style search (Shachter, 1998) from `x` that moves backward
to parents and forward to children, passing through a node in `z` only as a collider and
through a collider only when it is an ancestor of `x` or `z` (a sufficient criterion for
an open collider path). It visits each node at most once in each direction, so it runs
in $O(V + E)$ time, as NetworkX's `is_d_separator` does.

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~is_minimal_d_separator`) 

:   Check that no smaller set separates.

[](`~find_minimal_d_separator`) 

:   Find a minimal separator.

[](`~colliders`) 

:   The colliders that conditioning opens.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

In a chain `A` → `B` → `C`, conditioning on `B` blocks the path. In a collider
`A` → `B` ← `C`, it opens it:

::: {#f3f98cef .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

chain = ec.Graph.from_edges([("A", "B"), ("B", "C")], directed=True)
collider = ec.Graph.from_edges([("A", "B"), ("C", "B")], directed=True)

(
    chain.is_d_separator("A", "C", ["B"]),
    collider.is_d_separator("A", "C", ["B"]),
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(True, False)
```
:::
:::


### Reading independence from a diagram

`causal()` is a causal diagram of the risks for heart disease, with an arrow from each
cause to its effect. Exercise and Diet are d-separated given nothing, since their only
connection is the collider Weight. Conditioning on Weight, or on Blood pressure or Heart
disease, which it affects, opens that path:

::: {#dd8e7fe7 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
causal = ec.causal()

{
    tuple(given): causal.is_d_separator("Exercise", "Diet", given)
    for given in [[], ["Weight"], ["Heart disease"]]
}
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
{(): True, ('Weight',): False, ('Heart disease',): False}
```
:::
:::


Age reaches Heart disease only through Blood pressure, but conditioning on Blood
pressure alone doesn't separate them: Blood pressure is a collider on the path through
Genetics, in the accent color. Adding Genetics blocks it:

::: {#2946930e .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
(
    causal.is_d_separator("Age", "Heart disease", ["Blood pressure"]),
    causal.is_d_separator(
        "Age", "Heart disease", ["Blood pressure", "Genetics"]
    ),
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
```
(False, True)
```
:::
:::


::: {#cdbde480 .cell execution_count=4}
``` {.python .cell-code}
causal.plot(
    layout="pos",
    highlight=[("Age", "Blood pressure"), ("Genetics", "Blood pressure"),
               ("Genetics", "Heart disease")],
    label_by="label", height=360,
)
```

